From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Subject: [PATCH] ozone: hint BT.709 video as BT.709 when importing it through EGL

NativePixmapEGLBinding passes EGL_ITU_REC601_EXT as the YUV colour-space
hint for every NV12, YV12 and P010 import whose matrix is not BT.2020,
except full-range BT.709, which it hints as BT.709. The comment ties
that to ChromeOS, where limited-range frames may be promoted to DRM/KMS
overlays that are forced to BT.601. Linux composites video through this
import instead, so limited-range BT.709 content (most video) is
converted with the BT.601 matrix and its colours are shifted.

Hint BT.709 for limited-range BT.709 too on Linux.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
--- a/ui/ozone/common/native_pixmap_egl_binding.cc
+++ b/ui/ozone/common/native_pixmap_egl_binding.cc
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include "base/memory/scoped_refptr.h"
 #include "base/notreached.h"
 #include "base/numerics/safe_conversions.h"
+#include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "ui/gfx/linux/drm_util_linux.h"
 #include "ui/gl/gl_bindings.h"
 #include "ui/gl/gl_surface_egl.h"
@@ -166,7 +167,15 @@
           attrs.push_back(EGL_ITU_REC709_EXT);
           break;
         }
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+        // Linux composites video through this import (no DRM/KMS overlay with
+        // a forced BT.601 matrix), so limited-range BT.709 content needs the
+        // BT.709 matrix too.
+        attrs.push_back(EGL_ITU_REC709_EXT);
+        break;
+#else
         [[fallthrough]];
+#endif
       default:
         attrs.push_back(EGL_ITU_REC601_EXT);
     }
